roup that can be a good or a bad thing  right when one one obvious question  would be well with which group one could  have as a result of Honor more  solidarity towards their religious group  which might cause conflict within the  broader population as an example so is  there any way to start assessing in  terms of in an objective way the  positive and negative impacts of  differing degrees of Honor in a society  well I think a lot of that will depend  on the norms or the code of the  particular group in questions so one of  the things that is extremely flexible  across honor cultures of all sorts is  what are what are the behaviors that or  in character traits that will bring you  more honor and what are the character  traits or behaviors that will bring you  shame so they have these norms for  allocating honor within the group if  those norms are immoral norms you know  so something you mentioned honor  killings at if if the code within the  group is that a woman who is suspected  of having extramarital  Saxe the family will be shamed unless  they kill that woman you know that's a  terrible norm it's a morally atrocious  norm and so in that kind of community  the what honor causes people to do is  going to be something that we should  discourage I mean this is one of the  problems with honor is it doesn't really  place any limits inherently on what will  bring you honor and what will bring you  shame and so when those things are  things that are morally good then the  motivational power of Honor becomes a  huge advantage but when they're bad the  motivational power of Honor it becomes  something that we need to really worry  about so within the United States what  would be the sort of primary subcultures  or enclaves of society that are more  honor based so the military definitely  for one sometimes often explicitly the  Navy SEALs have a honor code that's  spelled out I think sports teams and  especially hockey teams and baseball  teams operate according to and 